          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETop%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ETop∞Grpd
            and those contexts containing it, the internal notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+groups+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} geometric homotopy
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
            subsume the usual ones (over well-behaved topological spaces). In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            also the notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} Chern-Weil theory
            subsume the usual ones. In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SynthDiff%E2%88%9EGrpd"} SynthDiff∞Grpd
            the internal notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
            subsumes the traditional one — and generalizes them to higher smooth geometry.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p We state the definition in several equivalent ways.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ExternalDefinition"} externally
                in the ambient context;
            %li
